I see the difference! In the OLD code (before the infinite loop fix), the EOC error path at line 240-242 was:
```c
*decode_error = PTLS_ERROR_BER_UNEXPECTED_EOC;
```
Without `byte_index = bytes_max;` and `break;`.

This means in the old code, when an EOC error occurred, the function would NOT break out of the while loop. It would continue to the next iteration, where `byte_index` is still the same value. This would cause an infinite loop.

But more importantly, in the old code, after the EOC error, the function would continue to the recursive call at line 254 (the else branch). Wait, no, the EOC error is in the `if` branch, not the `else` branch. After the error message, the code would fall through to the logging code at line 263-268, then loop back to the while condition.

But the current code (in the repo) already has the fix. So the vulnerability we're looking for must be something else.

Let me look at the current code one more time. Actually, wait. Let me re-read the current code at line 237-252:

```c
if (indefinite_length != 0 && bytes[byte_index] == 0) {
    if (byte_index + 2 > bytes_max || bytes[byte_index + 1] != 0) {
        byte_index =
            ptls_asn1_error_message("EOC: unexpected end of content", bytes_max, byte_index, level + 1, log_ctx);

        *decode_error = PTLS_ERROR_BER_UNEXPECTED_EOC;
        byte_index = bytes_max;
        break;
    } else {
        if (log_ctx != NULL) {
            ptls_asn1_print_indent(level, log_ctx);
            log_ctx->fn(log_ctx->ctx, "EOC\n");
        }
        byte_index += 2;
        break;
    }
}
```

In the current code, when we have an indefinite length and `bytes[byte_index] == 0`, we check for EOC. If `byte_index + 2 > bytes_max`, we set an error and break. If `byte_index + 2 <= bytes_max` and `bytes[byte_index + 1] == 0`, we have a valid EOC and break. If `bytes[byte_index + 1] != 0`, we set an error and break.

But what if `byte_index + 2 <= bytes_max` and `bytes[byte_index + 1] != 0`? Then we set an error and break. But wait, the condition is:
```c
if (byte_index + 2 > bytes_max || bytes[byte_index + 1] != 0) {
```

If `byte_index + 2 <= bytes_max` AND `bytes[byte_index + 1] == 0`, we go to the else branch (valid EOC). Otherwise, we go to the if branch (error).

But what if `byte_index + 2 <= bytes_max` and `bytes[byte_index + 1] != 0`? Then we go to the if branch (error). We set `*decode_error`, set `byte_index = bytes_max`, and break. This is fine.

But what if `byte_index + 2 > bytes_max`? Then we go to the if branch (error). We set `*decode_error`, set `byte_index = bytes_max`, and break. This is also fine.

So the EOC handling seems correct in the current code.

Let me think about this from yet another angle. What if the vulnerability is in `ptls_asn1_read_type` when `bytes_max` is 0, and the way to get `bytes_max = 0` is through the recursive call when `last_byte - byte_index = 0`?

But the while loop prevents this. UNLESS... there's a way to bypass the while loop.

Wait, what if `structure_bit` is 0 (primitive type)? Then the code goes to line 276-278:
```c
} else {
    ptls_asn1_dump_content(bytes, last_byte, byte_index, log_ctx);
    byte_index = last_byte;
}
```

No recursive call for primitive types. So the recursive call only happens for constructed types (structure_bit = 1).

For constructed types, the while loop checks `byte_index < last_byte`. If `last_byte == byte_index`, the loop doesn't execute.

But what if `last_byte < byte_index`? Then `last_byte - byte_index` wraps around. But `last_byte` is always >= `byte_index` as computed in `ptls_asn1_read_length`.

